 "notes": "Libretto by Luigi Illica and Giuseppe Giacosa (1896). Words follow the Ricordi vocal score's spelling and punctuation (Bordò, lippo, Allorausai printed as one run of syllables, salsiccie, un'olezzo, finchè, the print's „Guardare!,, and the dots after nostri, Lord and fuor). Schaunard imitates the English lord's broken Italian (“Guardare!”, “Voi suonare finchè quello morire!”); Lorito is the parrot; the Costituzional is the newspaper they use as a tablecloth. Shouted notes (gridato, printed without noteheads) keep their words. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, words sung on one note joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ, intervocalic s voiced. Translation literal, for meaning.",
